campaignTrail_temp.questions_json = [{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":1,"fields":{"description":"January 16, 2008: We knew we weren't at an advantage there, but the results from the Michigan primary are worse than expected. Barack Obama won big, the media are treating him and  John Edwards as the only plausible nominees. Our campaign is described as an ideological footnote at best, and an egotistical venture at worst. Nevada could be more competitive, what do you want the campaign to emphasize?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":31,"fields":{"description":"Obama and Edwards have had a much easier time attracting celebrity attention than you have, but we've got a few recognizable names willing to hit the trail. It won't win us the nomination, Dennis, but a little star power couldn't hurt. Who do you want out there with you?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":2,"fields":{"description":"Stepping off the campaign bus, you aren't greeted by our volunteers. Walking into the campaign office, you spot our staff and canvassers huddled together around a TV screen. Phones are ringing off the hook and nobody seems to notice. Barack Obama was shot and killed while leaving a campaign event in Elko, Nevada. The country is stunned and waiting for more details. Obviously, we will wait a bit to say something, but we need to draft a statement."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":3,"fields":{"description":"January 20, 2008: There are still so many questions left unanswered. The assassin was killed by Secret Service agents immediately after the killing. Journalists have already begun scavenging for a motive, with little more than the killer's MySpace page to go on. It's mostly nonsensical rants about \"the man\" and crappy poetry, but buried beneath all that they did find some quotes from Morton Downey Jr. This has led some to believe he was motivated by a right-wing ideology to commit the murder. Edwards is suspending his campaign momentarily to mourn, what do you think?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":4,"fields":{"description":"January 27, 2008: Most vigils for Barack Obama have remained peaceful, but several cities have seen scattered riots and urban unrest. It's not on the scale of the LA riots, but 3 law enforcement officials have been killed as well as a dozen or so civilians. It's more than bad enough that every network is showing broken windows and burning cars. A reporter asks you directly what you would say to people taking to the streets in anger. What do you say?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":5,"fields":{"description":"January 29, 2008: Edwards blew us out of the water in South Carolina by 29 points, carrying Black voters and much of Obama's former coalition. Reporters are already describing the nomination fight as effectively over. What is our response?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":6,"fields":{"description":"February 2, 2008: Edwards has now won Florida by double digits. Volunteers are leaving, donations are drying up, and even some of your supporters have privately resigned themselves to an Edwards presidency. We need to change course. What's our message?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":7,"fields":{"description":"Several reporters are quietly asking whether we have heard rumors about an extramarital affair involving John Edwards and a media consultant. Nothing has been substantiated, and nobody wants to appear to exploit a scandal. That said, with his wife Elizabeth being a cancer survivor this is extremely damaging if accurate. How should we proceed?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":8,"fields":{"description":"February 4, 2008: The affair allegation has broken into the open. Edwards denies it completely and accuses hostile media organizations of attempting to destroy the Democratic frontrunner. Someone in his campaign is talking, but nobody knows who yet. According to the press, it's someone who is highly credible. Reporters want to know whether you believe Edwards. Keep in mind, if he ends up as the nominee this could really backfire. Any ideas?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":9,"fields":{"description":" Evidence has emerged tying Edwards to the woman and to a child whose paternity is now under intense scrutiny. And get this: there are reports he used campaign funds to keep the woman quiet. This is beyond anything anyone anticipated. Edwards hasn't responded and is still campaigning in small venues, meanwhile ranking Democrats are begging both campaigns to avoid splitting the party over this. What do you say?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":10,"fields":{"description":"February 6, 2008: Edwards has withdrawn. Super Tuesday is over, and California was not close—you carried it by almost thirty points. Anti-war voters, anti-establishment Democrats, organized labor activists, and former Obama supporters are converging behind the only campaign left that speaks to their anger. Our campaign is a late bloomer to put it lightly, but right now you're the last man standing. We just have to make sure the DNC doesn't pull some stunts to knock us out. Right now, it's time to reintroduce our message to the people. What will you say?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":11,"fields":{"description":"February 2008: We've made massive gains with Edwards gone. Endorsements are pouring in. Hell, even Nader is in our corner. Aside from a small fringe voting uncommitted in protest, most Democrats see you as someone they can trust. Pundits have especially taken note of your wholesome relationship with your wife Elizabeth. It seems being ideologically consistent and principled is finally working in our favor. Now, we have to consider our general election strategy. Obviously, we need the Obama coalition. It can't hurt to make inroads with the more moderate voters who backed Edwards, too. What're your thoughts? "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":13,"fields":{"description":"March 2008: A reporter points out an important precedent. George W. Bush ran in 2000 promising a more humble foreign policy and warning against nation-building, then presided over Iraq, detention, torture allegations and an enormous expansion of executive power. Why should voters believe a President Kucinich would not discover his own reasons to abandon the foreign-policy restraints he campaigns on?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":14,"fields":{"description":"March 2008: Some activists are openly suggesting that Obama's assassination may have been part of a larger conspiracy. Jesse Ventura specifically compared the assassination to the Gulf of Tonkin incident due to what he says are inconsistencies in the official story. Normally, I'd say ignore this but this is spreading like wildfire online. No credible evidence has established that there was a larger motive, but it might be a good idea to establish our response early. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":32,"fields":{"description":"Howard Dean and Kathleen Sebelius want one last meeting before Denver. Nobody is threatening your nomination, but they want a few firm platform commitments and a united front for down-ballot Democrats. How hard do you bargain?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":33,"fields":{"description":"Your running mate has joined the ticket. How do you want to introduce the choice to the country?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":15,"fields":{"description":"Denver. You have done what almost nobody in the Democratic Party thought possible. Tonight you accept the nomination before delegates who are still deciding whether your victory is a moral awakening or a nervous breakdown. Millions of Americans who know you mostly as the anti-war congressman from Cleveland will hear you at length for the first time. What story do you tell them?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":16,"fields":{"description":"May 2008: The Republican nomination fight is finally over. Your opponent has been chosen."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":17,"fields":{"description":"May 2008: Several days after securing the Republican nomination, your opponent has announced a running mate."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":34,"fields":{"description":"Republican operatives have begun making hay over an appearance you had on Bill Maher's show last year. It seemed to mostly fly under the radar before you unexpectedly won the nomination, but it's more than just a little damaging. In the clip, you're asked if you'd give the order to take out Osama bin Laden should you become President and your administration has located him. You responded that you \"wouldn't use assassination as an instrument of policy\". Even in the clip, Maher is taken aback by this. This is extremely damaging, we need our arms around this thing before they completely control the narrative."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":18,"fields":{"description":"July 2008: Your opposition to the Iraq War is popular, but Republicans say your foreign policy amounts to retreat. How do you make the case for withdrawal?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":19,"fields":{"description":"July 2008: The Democratic convention is approaching. Party leaders want a speech that can reassure moderates without draining the energy that carried you through the primaries. What tone should you take?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":20,"fields":{"description":"August 2008: The convention bounce is smaller than hoped. {OPPONENT} still leads clearly, and much of the press treats your campaign as a historic curiosity with a predictable ending. What is the plan?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":21,"fields":{"description":"August 2008: The first presidential debate is approaching. Which contrast with {OPPONENT} should define the night?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":22,"fields":{"description":"September 15, 2008: You wake up to a dozen missed calls. One message from an adviser simply says, 'Turn on the television.' Lehman Brothers has filed for bankruptcy. Credit markets are freezing, Wall Street is panicking, and nobody, not your campaign, not the Republicans, not the White House, knows how far this goes. Every speech planned yesterday suddenly feels obsolete. What do you say first?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":23,"fields":{"description":"Congress has finally put a rescue bill in front of you. Democratic leaders tell you privately that if it fails and the markets seize up, a Kucinich vote against it will be replayed every hour until Election Day and almost certainly end in a landslide defeat. Your own supporters are furious that Wall Street is being rescued at all. How do you vote?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":24,"fields":{"description":"At a campaign stop in Michigan, a reporter asks whether your decades-long opposition to NAFTA and repeated calls for immediate withdrawal are still positions you stand by as the nominee. You've spent years calling for the United States to withdraw from the agreement. Would a President Kucinich actually do it?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":25,"fields":{"description":"The Republican campaign has released a new attack ad. Grainy footage from the 2007 Democratic debate shows you acknowledging that you once saw an unidentified flying object at Shirley MacLaine's home. It cuts between several of your more unconventional proposals before fading to black: 'DENNIS KUCINICH. PRESIDENT?' Cable news is looping it. What do you say?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":26,"fields":{"description":"At a town hall, a reporter brings up your support for transgender rights. You've called for protections based specifically on gender identity, which is pretty out of step with a lot of the country that isn't ready for same-sex marriage. Republicans say you're asking the country to move much further and faster than it's prepared to. What do you say?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":27,"fields":{"description":"October 2008: Your running mate is preparing for the vice-presidential debate. These debates are often somewhat forgettable, but in a close election they can be make or break. What role should they play?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":28,"fields":{"description":"October 2008: The final debate will focus on domestic policy. What should be your closing contrast with {OPPONENT}?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":12,"fields":{"description":"Late October 2008: The campaign has momentum, but our field operation is stretched thin and several Democratic officials are still keeping their distance. We have enough money for one final national push. What should it look like?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":29,"fields":{"description":"There are only a few days left, and the map is close enough that one good trip could matter. One sentimental detour could matter too, for the wrong reason. Where do you spend the final weekend?"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.question","pk":30,"fields":{"description":"Election Eve. There is nothing left to unveil and nobody left to persuade with another white paper. You get one final national address. What do you want people to believe this campaign was about?"}}];

campaignTrail_temp.answers_json = [{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":1,"fields":{"question":1,"description":"I am going to stay on the issues. I opposed this war before it was fashionable, and Nevada voters deserve to hear the economic and human cost of continuing it.","feedback":"The anti-war base appreciates the clarity, although the national press continues to treat the campaign as marginal. At least it may take a little wind out of Edwards's sails."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":2,"fields":{"question":1,"description":"I want to broaden the message. We should talk about health care, wages, and peace in practical terms. I am not going to pretend we are leading this race, but people deserve a reason to listen.","feedback":"This is disciplined and credible, but it does little to break through a race dominated by Obama and Edwards. Talking about the party platform this early isn't exactly optimistic, and people want to vote for a winner."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":3,"fields":{"question":1,"description":"This has to become a direct challenge to the political establishment. The press does not get to decide who is viable before voters do, and powerful interests have every reason to fear what we are proposing.","feedback":"Our people love it. Reporters, however, portray the campaign as angry and conspiratorial."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":4,"fields":{"question":1,"description":"We have to be realistic about our resources. I will keep fighting for these issues in Congress and on the ballot, but I am not going to burn the whole campaign pretending Nevada is something it is not.","feedback":"This conserves resources but confirms nearly every assumption about the campaign's irrelevance."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":5,"fields":{"question":2,"description":"Barack Obama represented the possibility that our country could become better than it had been. Today there is no campaign, only grief. My thoughts are with his family and with every person who believed in what he was trying to build.","feedback":"The response is mournful and humane. It lowers the temperature without erasing the anger many Democrats feel."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":6,"fields":{"question":2,"description":"I am asking everyone who supports this campaign to come together in grief and to trust the investigation unless facts give us reason not to. Nothing we say today should deepen fear or division.","feedback":"Most praise the restraint. There is a growing fringe who believe there's more to this incident than the media is telling us, and your call to \"trust\" the investigation leads to some wild speculation. Still, the vast majority of Americans feel the same as you do."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":7,"fields":{"question":2,"description":"I understand the anger people feel. America cannot keep losing people who challenge power before their work is finished. But grief has to become a commitment to change, not an excuse for hatred.","feedback":"The words strike a powerful chord with progressives and grieving Democrats, but non-friendly media accuse you of politicization. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":8,"fields":{"question":2,"description":"I do not want to turn mourning into political theater. I will release a brief statement, offer my condolences, and otherwise stay out of the cameras.","feedback":"The statement avoids exploitation, and right now that's probably what's most important."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":9,"fields":{"question":3,"description":"Everything stops for at least a week. I will attend the services quietly, and I want our supporters volunteering in Barack’s name rather than campaigning in mine.","feedback":"Well said, nobody can call you opportunistic. We lose some campaign time, but we'd lose way more support than we could've gained in that time by being out there anyway."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":10,"fields":{"question":3,"description":"We should honor the suspension, but I also want to speak directly to our supporters online. Barack’s work cannot simply disappear because he is gone.","feedback":"Some people did like this, but overall it just came off as cheap and opportunistic. You didn't mention Edwards directly, but the implication seemed to be that you were Obama's true successor. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":11,"fields":{"question":3,"description":"Campaign activity should stop, but our movement cannot disappear. We can respect this moment and still organize quietly so that one candidate does not inherit the entire race by default.","feedback":"Organizers appreciate the realism. Reporters who discover the private activity accuse the campaign of being two-faced."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":12,"fields":{"question":3,"description":"At some point the country still has to choose a nominee. I am prepared to resume campaigning before Edwards does if that is what it takes to keep this race from becoming a coronation.","feedback":"The move looks opportunistic and sharply damages your standing during a national tragedy."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":13,"fields":{"question":4,"description":"I understand the anger. But Barack believed this country could become better than this. We honor him by organizing, voting and building that country—not by hurting people who had nothing to do with his death.","feedback":"Restraint helps with party leaders and former Obama supporters, though Edwards keeps gaining ground and defining the narrative. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":14,"fields":{"question":4,"description":"There can be no justice without peace and no peace without justice. I am asking everyone who loved Barack Obama to reject violence while demanding that our government answer the deeper despair behind it.","feedback":"The appeal for calm is decent, but the broader language about justice leaves some voters wondering if you meant it"}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":15,"fields":{"question":4,"description":"I will condemn violence, but I am not going to pretend a broken window is a greater national emergency than war, poverty and a young senator being murdered. We need some perspective.","feedback":"The comparison is pure Kucinich: morally forceful and almost guaranteed to start an argument. Some progressives applaud you for refusing to let property damage eclipse bigger problems, poverty, and Obama's murder; others think you just handed Republicans a brutal soundbite. Remember, if these riots get worse we could be blamed."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":16,"fields":{"question":4,"description":"Anyone destroying another person’s home or business should stop. Full stop. We can mourn Barack and demand change without giving anyone an excuse to answer grief with destruction.","feedback":"This lands about where you wanted it to. You condemn the destruction without discounting people's anger."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":17,"fields":{"question":5,"description":"I congratulate Senator Edwards, but the Democratic Party still deserves a real debate over war, poverty, health care, and corporate power. I intend to give it one.","feedback":"Edwards wins the news cycle, but your campaign retains dignity and a reason to continue. The defeat hurts, but the cheers of your diehard supporters help soften the blow. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":18,"fields":{"question":5,"description":"No candidate inherits Barack Obama’s voters. They are citizens, not political property, and every one of us has to earn their support.","feedback":"This resonates with voters uncomfortable with the media's instant coronation of Edwards, but a lot of people sadly tune it out."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":19,"fields":{"question":5,"description":"I am going to draw a sharper contrast with Senator Edwards. A campaign financed and advised by the same political class cannot simply declare itself the vehicle for change.","feedback":"The attack creates attention and reinforces your anti-establishment identity, but alienates many former Obama supporters."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":20,"fields":{"question":5,"description":"I know what the numbers say. If there is a way to secure real commitments on war, health care, and economic justice, I am willing to discuss what unity might require.","feedback":"Some in the media compliment your realism. Most of your remaining volunteers do not."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":21,"fields":{"question":6,"description":"This campaign was never built for easy moments. I am staying in because somebody has to speak plainly about the war machine, corporate power, and the people Washington keeps leaving behind.","feedback":"The campaign remains bleak, but the speech prevents an immediate collapse."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":22,"fields":{"question":6,"description":"If the voters will not make us the nominee, then we can still be the conscience of this party. I want delegates, platform commitments, and enough leverage that these issues cannot be ignored.","feedback":"This sounds more like a movement preparing to lose than a campaign preparing to win."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":23,"fields":{"question":6,"description":"If voters believe Senator Edwards is safer, then we have to show them that conviction and stability are not opposites. I can adjust how we present this campaign without abandoning why we got into this race.","feedback":"The pivot reassures some insiders and deeply disappoints the people still keeping the campaign alive."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":24,"fields":{"question":6,"description":"I am not interested in dragging this out for vanity. If there is no path after Super Tuesday, we should preserve what credibility we have and carry these causes into the next fight.","feedback":"Withdrawal chatter becomes the only story."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":25,"fields":{"question":7,"description":"I am not touching rumors about somebody’s private life. If we cannot beat John Edwards on war, health care, and the economy, gossip is not going to save us.","feedback":"The decision earns respect and protects the campaign if the rumor proves false."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":26,"fields":{"question":7,"description":"I will ask John privately whether there is anything the party needs to know. We will not spread unverified claims, but I am not going to let Democrats walk blind into something Republicans already know.","feedback":"The private approach is responsible, but you never get so much as a message on an answering machine back from Edwards."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":27,"fields":{"question":7,"description":"All I will say publicly is that any Democratic nominee has to survive scrutiny before the Republicans do it for us. I am not going to repeat rumors I cannot prove.","feedback":"This is cautious but politically meaningful. Reporters begin treating the rumor as a legitimate electability issue."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":28,"fields":{"question":7,"description":"I know this is ugly, but I also know what is at stake. Let friendly voices raise the questions while I keep my distance from the story.","feedback":"The tactic spreads the story quickly and leaves fingerprints close enough for the press to notice. Nothing they can prove though luckily. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":29,"fields":{"question":8,"description":"I will not convict anyone through headlines. Senator Edwards deserves an opportunity to answer with evidence, and I am going to keep campaigning on the issues.","feedback":"The answer is fair-minded. If the allegation grows, you will not be blamed for exploiting it."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":30,"fields":{"question":8,"description":"His denial is now a question of public trust. Before Democrats nominate him, he owes the country a complete and truthful accounting.","feedback":"This raises the stakes without indulging the most lurid coverage."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":31,"fields":{"question":8,"description":"This is exactly why I have tried to make this campaign about systems and policies rather than personalities. I have nothing more to add.","feedback":"The refusal to engage gives us some respectability with people who'd otherwise hate us, but Edwards controls the response."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":32,"fields":{"question":8,"description":"The press has protected Senator Edwards for too long. I want an independent investigation, and I want to know why so many people in politics and media apparently decided voters did not deserve the truth.","feedback":"This frustrates our people, even some of your most die-hard supporters. Nobody likes the media, but this is way too much."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":33,"fields":{"question":9,"description":"This is a tragedy for his family and a crisis for the party. John has to tell the truth now, and Democrats cannot excuse deception simply because we are afraid of losing an election.","feedback":"The response looks presidential and makes dishonesty—not sex—the central issue. One thing though: maybe don't reference JFK when talking about a sex scandal."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":34,"fields":{"question":9,"description":"There is no reason for me to get into the mud. If even half of this is true, John has enough problems without Dennis Kucinich piling on.","feedback":"Party leaders appreciate the restraint, although some do wish you'd be more forceful here. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":35,"fields":{"question":9,"description":"This is bigger than one man’s scandal. We keep manufacturing celebrity candidates with donors, consultants, and media narratives, and then everyone acts shocked when the image turns out not to be the person.","feedback":"You're right, Dennis. Sadly, being right doesn't count for a whole lot when you look this opportunistic. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":36,"fields":{"question":9,"description":"John Edwards should withdraw. If he carries this into the convention, Republicans will turn a Democratic scandal into a national landslide.","feedback":"You're not saying anything Democrats all over the country aren't already thinking, but mentioning electability reminds people of your own electoral disadvantages. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":37,"fields":{"question":10,"description":"This victory belongs to everyone who was told that peace, universal health care and economic justice were impossible. We are going to carry those causes forward together.","feedback":"The nomination becomes yours without requiring you to abandon the tone that carried the campaign through tragedy."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":38,"fields":{"question":10,"description":"We all know how different this race was supposed to be. We have grieved, we have been shaken, and now I am asking Democrats to turn that grief into a campaign worthy of the people we lost.","feedback":"The speech forges intense unity among progressives and many former Obama supporters. I know some of the party bosses are sweating, but in a moment like this one your authentic persona is moving."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":39,"fields":{"question":10,"description":"I want every Democrat who supported Edwards or Obama to know there is a place for them here. I understand the burden of this nomination, and I will not let any of you down.","feedback":"The party begins consolidating quickly. Some core supporters worry that caution is replacing conviction. Still, in a moment like this one there's nothing wrong with unity."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":41,"fields":{"question":11,"description":"I understand the impulse to reinvent ourselves, but Democrats value this campaign because we have been consistent. I am not going to throw that away now. Come November, we are still going to need to bring this party together.","feedback":"This excites progressives but can deepen concern among voters who already see the campaign as too ideological. A fractured party is much harder to mobilize. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":42,"fields":{"question":11,"description":"We can position some of our policies in different terms. Foreign policy however is something I will not budge on.","feedback":"Democrats are ready for a new direction, but your proposed Department of Peace is a bridge too far for some. We may want to figure out how to sell that one too. "}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":43,"fields":{"question":11,"description":"Democrats also need to remember what losing this election means. After Iraq, torture, illegal surveillance and everything else, I'm not interested in promising the Bush administration in advance that everybody gets to walk away without answering any questions.","feedback":"Well, that certainly answers the question about whether the campaign intends to calm down. Anti-war activists like the accountability language; nervous Democrats hear the first hint that a Kucinich presidency could begin with a constitutional brawl."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":45,"fields":{"question":12,"description":"I want the largest volunteer canvassing operation we can build in the industrial Midwest. Let union members, organizers, and families facing poverty make the case door to door.","feedback":"This is expensive and unglamorous, but it gives the campaign a real organization in the states most likely to decide the election."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":46,"fields":{"question":12,"description":"Buy the national address. I want to look into the camera and explain why the same people who dismissed our economic warnings are now asking working Americans to rescue them.","feedback":"The address is one of the strongest moments of the campaign. Your warnings about Wall Street now sound prophetic rather than ideological."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":47,"fields":{"question":12,"description":"We need to reassure moderate Democrats and independents. Put respected governors and members of Congress beside me and show the country that this campaign is prepared to govern.","feedback":"The endorsements calm some nervous voters, although the campaign loses a little of the insurgent energy that made it distinctive."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":48,"fields":{"question":12,"description":"I want to go directly at the Republican ticket. Every closing advertisement should ask whether America really wants four more years of war, deregulation, and government by corporate insiders.","feedback":"The attacks energize Democrats, but the closing tone feels relentlessly negative to voters who were beginning to reconsider you."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":49,"fields":{"question":13,"description":"My record is not a campaign brochure. I opposed this war before it began, I opposed the Patriot Act, and I have spent years voting against the expansion of presidential war power.","feedback":"Republicans will of course call this naive, but it's probably the best way to reassure voters who see your rise as a media fad."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":50,"fields":{"question":13,"description":"No president should be trusted on his word alone, not George Bush and not Dennis Kucinich. Congress must reclaim its war powers, the courts must remain independent, and the public has a right to know what its government is doing.","feedback":"Institutional restraint is a serious answer. People like hearing a presidential candidate say that even his own power should be checked, though it is not the kind of answer that electrifies a rally."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":51,"fields":{"question":13,"description":"I am not merely promising to avoid another Iraq. If crimes were committed in the conduct of this war, my administration will cooperate with lawful international investigations rather than place American officials above the law.","feedback":"This detonates across cable news. Progressives applaud but Republicans immediately portray you as preparing international prosecutions of an outgoing American administration."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":52,"fields":{"question":13,"description":"The best safeguard is a foreign policy that uses diplomacy before force. That's why I proposed a Department of Peace: preventing war deserves an institution of its own.","feedback":"The Department of Peace gives you a coherent theory of prevention rather than merely withdrawal. It also reminds skeptical voters of one of the proposals Republicans find easiest to ridicule."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":53,"fields":{"question":14,"description":"No. There is no credible evidence of a conspiracy, and grief cannot become an excuse for reckless accusations.","feedback":"The unequivocal rejection reassures party leaders, reporters, and many ordinary voters. Some conspiratorial activists are furious, but you make clear that grief is not evidence."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":54,"fields":{"question":14,"description":"I have seen no evidence of a conspiracy. I also understand why Americans distrust institutions, and that is why I support complete transparency about what happened.","feedback":"This is a pretty safe bet. You acknowledge why people are suspicious without endorsing the conspiracy itself, and the transparency pledge gives them somewhere responsible to go."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":55,"fields":{"question":14,"description":"I am not endorsing rumors, but an independent bipartisan commission can give the public an investigation it trusts. That is the responsible way to answer legitimate questions.","feedback":"A commission sounds nice, but it keeps the story alive and gives the conspiracy another week of oxygen. You gain credibility with some skeptics while increasing the sense that unanswered questions surround the campaign."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":56,"fields":{"question":14,"description":"I'm not going to sneer at people for asking questions. Powerful institutions don't always tell the truth, just look at 9/11. People deserve answers, not ridicule.","feedback":"This is exactly the kind of answer Republicans were hoping for. Ventura loves the 9/11 comment, which is not exactly the endorsement we needed."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":57,"fields":{"question":15,"description":"This election is about people who work for a living and have watched their jobs, homes, health care, and security sacrificed to an economy written for Wall Street. I intend to put working people back at the center of their government.","feedback":"The economic-populist speech gives skeptical viewers a recognizable reason for your candidacy. It is still left-wing, but the language of work, homes, and corporate power travels much farther than the caricature of Dennis Kucinich."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":58,"fields":{"question":15,"description":"We cannot repair America while pretending this war has not damaged our country and our standing in the world. I will end the occupation of Iraq, restore constitutional government, and make peace an organizing principle of American power.","feedback":"You sound completely like yourself, but nervous voters also hear a nominee promising a dramatic break with the foreign-policy establishment."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":59,"fields":{"question":15,"description":"I know millions of Democrats did not expect me to be standing here. I want to earn their confidence. We can be bold without being reckless, principled without being divided, and build a government worthy of the trust we are asking for tonight.","feedback":"The unity speech reassures elected Democrats and some independents. A few activists think you have gone soft, but the convention looks more like a governing party and less like an insurgent takeover."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":60,"fields":{"question":15,"description":"People know my positions. Tonight I want them to know why I hold them. I want to tell America where I came from, what my family went through, and why I have spent my life believing government should stand with people who do not have anyone else standing with them.","feedback":"This is the surprise of the convention. Your account of growing up poor, your family's repeated moves, and Sister Roberta's help gives viewers a reason to understand where your politics came from. For one night, even skeptical commentators admit they saw a side of Dennis Kucinich they had never bothered to learn about."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":61,"fields":{"question":16,"description":"Now we know who we are running against. I want to draw the contrast plainly and immediately.","feedback":"Moving immediately to contrast keeps the campaign aggressive and energized, though it risks making the Republican reveal feel like another ideological fight before voters have even met the nominee."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":62,"fields":{"question":16,"description":"I have disagreements with him, but I am not interested in caricatures. Let us find out what he actually intends to do with the country.","feedback":"The restraint plays well. You deny Republicans the easy claim that you will caricature anyone to your right and buy yourself time to define the actual matchup."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":63,"fields":{"question":16,"description":"Good. I would rather run against the Republican record than spend the summer pretending this election is about personalities.","feedback":"Running against the Republican record rather than the personality is a solid general-election frame. It keeps George Bush in the room without making the new nominee seem irrelevant."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":64,"fields":{"question":16,"description":"Nobody should congratulate himself yet. Anyone who survived that Republican primary knows how to campaign, and we have work to do.","feedback":"This is cautious but smart. Whoever survived the Republican primary is not going to collapse just because Democrats find the ticket strange; your staff appreciates that you are treating the opponent seriously."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":65,"fields":{"question":17,"description":"Put the whole ticket on the stage. I am ready to debate them on war, health care, trade, and the economy.","feedback":"This keeps the focus on substantive contrast and makes the vice-presidential choice part of the larger argument about war, health care, and the economy."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":66,"fields":{"question":17,"description":"I want voters to ask why this person was chosen. If the answer is geography or a simple balance, the nominee’s judgment should be questioned.","feedback":"Questioning the political logic of the pick can work if the choice really does look like a gimmick. If the running mate has an obvious governing rationale, the line can sound like reaching."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":67,"fields":{"question":17,"description":"I am not going to insult somebody simply for accepting a vice-presidential nomination. We will challenge the record and let the public judge.","feedback":"This is disciplined and presidential. You give up the chance for an immediate punch, but you also avoid looking petty about a person most voters barely know yet."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":68,"fields":{"question":17,"description":"Every running mate tells you something about the nominee’s judgment. We should take the choice seriously and make Republicans defend what it says about their ticket.","feedback":"Treating the pick as a judgment test is a strong frame because it lets the running mate matter without pretending the vice-presidential nominee is the center of the election."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":69,"fields":{"question":18,"description":"I will bring our troops home on a firm timetable. An occupation without an end is not a strategy, and asking more Americans to die for one does not make us safer.","feedback":"A firm timetable pleases the anti-war coalition and gives the country a clear endpoint. Security-minded voters still worry that you are announcing the schedule to America's enemies."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":70,"fields":{"question":18,"description":"Our troops have done everything asked of them. Lower violence gives us the opportunity to leave responsibly, and I intend to take it.","feedback":"Crediting the troops and using lower violence as the reason to leave is much easier for nervous voters to accept. Some anti-war supporters dislike how much credit you give the surge narrative."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":71,"fields":{"question":18,"description":"Congress surrendered too much of its constitutional responsibility. I want it to reclaim the war power and force an orderly end to this occupation.","feedback":"Putting Congress at the center sounds constitutional rather than pacifist. It helps with voters worried about presidential overreach, though it is less emotionally satisfying to the movement."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":72,"fields":{"question":18,"description":"I will end the occupation, and I will not replace it with permanent American bases. Iraq has to recover its sovereignty and America has to recover its judgment.","feedback":"Rejecting permanent bases makes the distinction from the foreign-policy establishment unmistakable. It also maximizes Republican attacks that a Kucinich administration would abandon American leverage in the region."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":73,"fields":{"question":19,"description":"Give the movement the speech it earned: peace, health care, labor and economic democracy.","feedback":"The movement gets exactly the convention speech it wanted. Enthusiasm surges, while party moderates again wonder whether anyone is being invited into the coalition who was not already there."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":74,"fields":{"question":19,"description":"Sound presidential. Keep the principles, but make reassurance the theme.","feedback":"Reassurance is the politically strongest route with anxious voters. The downside is that activists who waited years for a Kucinich nomination hear a speech designed partly to calm people who opposed them."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":75,"fields":{"question":19,"description":"Make party unity the message and invite skeptical Democrats back into the tent.","feedback":"Party unity helps congressional Democrats and steadies donors. It is not especially memorable, but a boring convention can be useful when the nominee himself is the thing voters consider unusual."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":76,"fields":{"question":19,"description":"Turn the convention into an indictment of the Bush years and the system behind them.","feedback":"The indictment of the Bush years lands hard with Democrats. The risk is that after a year of national trauma, some voters hear anger where they wanted steadiness."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":77,"fields":{"question":20,"description":"I am not going to chase the horse race every morning. We will organize, speak plainly, and trust that this country is more volatile than the pundits understand.","feedback":"Ignoring the horse race keeps your organizers from panicking, but it does nothing by itself to answer the central problem: most voters still do not think you can win."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":78,"fields":{"question":20,"description":"We need to broaden this campaign. I want governors, senators, mayors, labor leaders, and people with governing experience standing beside us.","feedback":"Putting recognizable Democratic figures beside you makes the campaign look larger than one congressman and his movement. It costs a little insurgent mystique and gains a lot of institutional credibility."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":79,"fields":{"question":20,"description":"Then let us answer the competence question directly. I will show the country who would serve in this administration and what we intend to do on the first day.","feedback":"Answering the competence question directly is useful. Cabinet names, governing plans, and a concrete first hundred days make it harder to dismiss the campaign as merely expressive."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":80,"fields":{"question":20,"description":"The press does not get to declare an election over before the voters have held it. I am going to say that, and then I am going back to the issues they keep trying to turn into a sideshow.","feedback":"Complaints about media treatment resonate with your supporters, but they also risk confirming the press's favorite story about you: that the campaign blames the system whenever voters remain skeptical."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":81,"fields":{"question":21,"description":"I want Iraq and judgment to be the dividing line. The people who were wrong when it mattered do not get to call caution weakness now.","feedback":"Iraq is your clearest judgment argument and the contrast is authentic. It works best against a hawkish Republican and less well if voters are already exhausted by foreign-policy debate."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":82,"fields":{"question":21,"description":"I want to talk about wages, health care, trade, and corporate power. If Republicans want to defend an economy that works this well for Wall Street and this badly for everybody else, let them.","feedback":"Economics broadens the debate beyond your anti-war identity and gives working-class voters a reason to reconsider you on familiar ground."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":83,"fields":{"question":21,"description":"I do not have to surrender my principles to show that I can be calm, prepared, and presidential. Let them throw the labels while I answer the questions.","feedback":"Looking calm and presidential may be less exciting than landing punches, but for a nominee burdened by questions about temperament it can be worth more than a viral moment."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":84,"fields":{"question":21,"description":"I will make him defend the Bush record all night. This election is not happening in a vacuum, and Republicans do not get to wash eight years off their hands.","feedback":"Forcing the Republican to defend Bush is effective if the nominee cannot create distance. Against someone who has publicly broken with Bush, the attack can look stale."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":85,"fields":{"question":22,"description":"This isn't the time to tell everyone I told you so. People are scared. Tell them what we need to do in the next forty-eight hours to protect their jobs, savings and homes, then we'll talk about how we got here.","feedback":"This is exactly the tone nervous voters wanted. Your old warnings about concentrated financial power are suddenly being replayed everywhere, and resisting the urge to gloat makes them sound less like protest rhetoric and more like foresight."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":86,"fields":{"question":22,"description":"This is the bill for decades of deregulation. Wall Street gambled with the economy, Washington let them do it, and now working people are being handed the check.","feedback":"For once, the country is in a mood to hear it. Financial reporters call the speech ideological, but that word isn't hurting you like it did six months ago. Still, some frightened voters wanted reassurance before a lecture about who caused the fire."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":87,"fields":{"question":22,"description":"Protect deposits, payrolls, mortgages and the credit people need to keep businesses running. But there will be no blank check for the executives who helped create this mess.","feedback":"That's difficult to caricature as either doing nothing or simply bailing out Wall Street. For the first time, a lot of middle-class voters who dismissed you months ago are listening closely."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":88,"fields":{"question":22,"description":"Say very little until we know more. A presidential candidate shouldn't improvise during a financial panic.","feedback":"The caution is defensible, but the moment is moving faster than we are. You still gain simply because the political landscape has shifted toward issues you've talked about for years, but we leave a lot of that opportunity on the table."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":89,"fields":{"question":23,"description":"I'll vote yes if homeowners get direct relief, executives lose the golden parachutes, and the public receives equity or another real return for the risk we're taking. If taxpayers rescue these firms, taxpayers get something back.","feedback":"This is the compromise most of our coalition can live with. You're willing to stop a collapse, but only if homeowners get help and taxpayers aren't simply writing Wall Street a check. Even some Republicans admit that's a reasonable demand."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":90,"fields":{"question":23,"description":"I don't like this bill, but a complete credit collapse would hurt people who never set foot on Wall Street. I'll vote yes, demand hard oversight, and come back for structural reform when the immediate fire is contained.","feedback":"Well, Democratic leaders are certainly happy. This calms some of the panic about whether you'd actually act in a crisis, but a lot of our longtime supporters are pissed. They didn't come this far with Dennis Kucinich to watch him vote for a Wall Street bailout."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":91,"fields":{"question":23,"description":"No. Wall Street made these bets, Wall Street collected the profits, and now they want a frightened Congress to hand the losses to everybody else. I will vote no. If they want public money, they can come back with a bill that protects homeowners and gives the public something in return.","feedback":"Your supporters cheer, and nobody can accuse you of abandoning what you've preached for years. But now every market drop comes with footage of the Democratic nominee voting no. Fair or not, you've just taken some personal ownership of whatever happens next."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":92,"fields":{"question":23,"description":"Tell the leadership they don't have my vote yet. If they need the Democratic nominee to help pass this, then they can put homeowner relief, public equity and serious conditions into the bill before I walk onto the floor.","feedback":"They need you more than you need them right now. Leadership gives ground on homeowner relief and oversight rather than risk the Democratic nominee publicly walking away. For once, being difficult actually pays off."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":93,"fields":{"question":24,"description":"Notify Canada and Mexico that we are reopening the agreement immediately. If we cannot get enforceable labor and environmental standards and a fairer deal for workers, the United States should be prepared to withdraw.","feedback":"Nobody can accuse you of hiding the ball. Labor activists in the industrial Midwest are ecstatic, while business groups immediately begin warning about supply chains, exports and a trade shock."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":94,"fields":{"question":24,"description":"Renegotiate it with withdrawal on the table. I don't need to announce the outcome before negotiations begin, but our trading partners need to know the status quo is not permanent.","feedback":"Labor likes hearing that withdrawal is still on the table, while moderates hear something closer to a negotiation than an ultimatum. You keep most of the upside without scaring nearly as many people."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":95,"fields":{"question":24,"description":"I still think NAFTA was a mistake. But if the banking system is already freezing, I am not going to deliberately create another shock in the same week. Enforce every labor provision we can now, stabilize the economy, then come back to the agreement from a position of strength.","feedback":"Reasonable is exactly what worries some of your oldest labor supporters. You've spent years saying workers are always told to wait until conditions are better. Now, with the presidency in sight, you are asking them to wait too."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":96,"fields":{"question":24,"description":"The deeper problem is that workers have too little power regardless of which trade agreement is in force. I will concentrate on unions, industrial investment, wages, and enforcement rather than make withdrawal the centerpiece of the first year.","feedback":"Editorial boards appreciate the shift toward domestic industrial policy. A meaningful number of working-class voters think you just gave away one of the clearest reasons they trusted you."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":97,"fields":{"question":25,"description":"Well, I did see one. It does not mean it was from outer space. It was unidentified, and that makes it a UFO. That is all I ever said, and people should understand what the word actually means.","feedback":"You're technically correct, Dennis. That's not helping. We've now spent another news cycle explaining the definition of 'UFO,' and Republicans couldn't be happier."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":98,"fields":{"question":25,"description":"You know, more Americans have seen UFOs than approve of the Bush administration.","feedback":"Okay, that's funny. Unfortunately, everybody else thinks so too. The clip is everywhere by lunchtime, which means another full day of Dennis Kucinich and UFOs on television. Funny doesn't necessarily mean helpful."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":99,"fields":{"question":25,"description":"I saw something I could not explain, and 'UFO' was the best word I had for it. There were other witnesses too. If people think I am inventing this, they can corroborate what happened.","feedback":"Dennis, no. The witnesses confirm that you saw something, and now our presidential campaign appears to be assembling witnesses to a UFO sighting in the middle of a financial crisis. This is considerably worse."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":100,"fields":{"question":25,"description":"This is ridiculous. Anyone who watches the debate clip can watch the rest of what I said. Beyond that, I am not spending a financial crisis discussing a light I saw years ago.","feedback":"That's about as well as we were getting out of this. You refuse to feed the story and some voters recognize the ad for what it is. But the footage is real, Dennis, and for people already wondering whether you're a little too unconventional for the presidency, it fits the picture Republicans have spent months drawing."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":101,"fields":{"question":26,"description":"Transgender Americans are entitled to the same dignity and protection as everyone else. If Washington isn't ready to say that yet, then Washington ought to catch up.","feedback":"There's no ambiguity here. Civil-rights groups and progressive activists love it. A lot of culturally moderate voters, including plenty of Democrats, aren't anywhere near you on this yet."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":102,"fields":{"question":26,"description":"Nobody should lose a job, a home, or basic legal protection because of who they are. I want federal nondiscrimination protections, and I think we can explain that principle without turning every cultural disagreement into a national crusade.","feedback":"That's much easier to sell. You're still taking a position well outside where most politicians are willing to go, but jobs, housing and equal treatment give skeptical voters something concrete to think about."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":103,"fields":{"question":26,"description":"My principle is equality, but not every question has to be dictated from Washington at once. I would focus federal action on clear discrimination and allow states and communities room on some of the newer policy questions.","feedback":"Well, that's a real moderation. It helps with voters who think you're moving too fast, but some of our supporters are pretty disappointed. They backed you precisely because Dennis Kucinich wasn't supposed to wait for an issue to become politically safe."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":104,"fields":{"question":26,"description":"If being ahead of Washington means saying transgender Americans are entitled to the same dignity as everyone else, then Washington ought to catch up.","feedback":"There is no ambiguity at all. Progressives adore the answer; Republicans have the clip on television before the town hall is over."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":105,"fields":{"question":27,"description":"I want my running mate to make the case aggressively where I cannot always do it myself, but the attacks have to be rooted in the opponent's actual record rather than performance for its own sake.","feedback":"An aggressive running mate can prosecute the case effectively, but whether it works depends heavily on who is standing across the stage. With Gravel in particular, aggression can become the story instead of the Republican record."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":106,"fields":{"question":27,"description":"The first goal is reassurance. Americans should leave that debate believing the person beside me could govern tomorrow if circumstances required it.","feedback":"It's not flashy, but this is probably what nervous voters wanted to see. Your running mate spends the night looking prepared for the job rather than trying to win the morning headlines. It sacrifices some attack power in exchange for showing voters that someone else on the ticket looks ready to govern."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":107,"fields":{"question":27,"description":"I want the contrast focused on the Republican running mate's record and vulnerabilities. We do not need gratuitous aggression when facts will do the work.","feedback":"Focusing on the Republican running mate can expose a real weakness, but an overly personal performance can make your own ticket look obsessed with the understudy."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":108,"fields":{"question":27,"description":"I chose this person because I trust their judgment. I will prepare them thoroughly and then let them sound like themselves rather than like a committee wrote every sentence.","feedback":"You certainly get an authentic performance. With Paul or Gravel especially, authenticity can be a double-edged sword — the campaign staff spends much of the night wondering what is going to come out of their mouth next. With Paul or Gravel, the campaign has very little control over where the answer goes once the debate starts."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":109,"fields":{"question":28,"description":"I want the final debate to come back to one question: who does this government work for? My answer is the people who work, raise families, pay taxes, and keep getting told to wait.","feedback":"This is probably your cleanest closing argument of the debate. You sound less like a protest candidate and more like someone asking ordinary voters to judge whose side the government has been on."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":110,"fields":{"question":28,"description":"I will make the practical case: health care people can afford, jobs that cannot be shipped away without consequence, and a government competent enough to protect the economy.","feedback":"Jobs and wages are a natural place to close in the middle of the crash. You sound less exotic when the argument is simply who will rebuild the economy."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":111,"fields":{"question":28,"description":"I want to show the country that change does not have to mean chaos. We can break with the Bush years and still govern carefully, lawfully, and competently.","feedback":"Competence is exactly the vulnerability Republicans want you talking about, but meeting it directly can be valuable. A steady performance matters more than the policy detail here."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":112,"fields":{"question":28,"description":"I will make him defend the system that produced this crisis. If that sounds ideological, so be it; millions of Americans are living with the consequences.","feedback":"You put the Republican on defense over the crash and keep returning to the people who lost jobs, homes, and savings while Washington protected the institutions at the center of it. The attack is sharp, though nervous voters may still wonder whether anger at the system is the same thing as a plan to run it."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":113,"fields":{"question":29,"description":"Put me where the industrial map is closest. I want to spend the final days in the communities that have paid the highest price for bad trade policy and this economic collapse.","feedback":"Camping in the industrial Midwest is conventional but sensible. If the election is really going to turn on Michigan, Ohio, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, this is where every hour belongs."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":114,"fields":{"question":29,"description":"Send me wherever the numbers say one more visit can actually move electoral votes. Sentiment is not a strategy this late.","feedback":"Defending normally Democratic states feels humiliating, but losing one of them because the campaign went hunting for a miracle would be worse."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":115,"fields":{"question":29,"description":"I want to close in the states where our volunteer movement is strongest. A campaign like ours wins by turning people out who are normally treated as an afterthought.","feedback":"This is a bet on the people who carried you this far. The strongest volunteer organizations squeeze out a little more turnout and give the final weekend some genuine energy, though concentrating on your best-organized states means leaving a few tempting battlegrounds mostly to the television ads."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":116,"fields":{"question":29,"description":"Use my running mate where he or she can do the most good, and let me concentrate on the places where voters still need to hear directly from the nominee.","feedback":"Using the running mate geographically is smart when their state or demographic strengths matter. With a polarizing running mate, sending them somewhere merely because they are available can do more harm than good."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":117,"fields":{"question":30,"description":"I will close exactly where I began: peace, health care, economic justice, and a government that belongs to its people. Nobody should vote for me without knowing what I intend to change.","feedback":"This is the purest closing statement of the movement that nominated you. If the country is prepared to take the leap, nobody can say it misunderstood the mandate."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":118,"fields":{"question":30,"description":"I want the last message to be about trust and steadiness. America has had enough fear. I will change the direction of this country without treating half of it as an enemy.","feedback":"Trust and steadiness are the strongest reassurance themes available on Election Eve. Some activists find the closing message almost disappointingly conventional."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":119,"fields":{"question":30,"description":"I want to speak to people who feel politically homeless. You do not have to agree with every plank of this platform to believe the country needs honesty, peace, and a government less obedient to money.","feedback":"Speaking to politically homeless voters broadens the coalition without pretending ideological differences vanished. It is especially useful on a cross-party ticket."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":120,"fields":{"question":30,"description":"I will end on the economy. The people who told working Americans that nothing could change are now asking those same Americans to save the system. We can rebuild it on different terms.","feedback":"The populist close is unmistakably yours and lands well in the economic climate. Republicans answer that the rhetoric still does not make an unconventional nominee less risky."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":121,"fields":{"question":31,"description":"Let's get Willie Nelson. He's loved by people who probably don't agree on much else, and nobody's going to accuse us of trying too hard to look cool with Willie standing there.","feedback":"It's a nice event. Willie draws a crowd, gets plenty of smiles and even brings some Democrats who hadn't given you much thought before. Nothing earth-shattering, but we'll take it."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":122,"fields":{"question":31,"description":"Sean Penn's been outspoken against this war from the beginning. Get him out here with me. He's famous, he's passionate, and at least nobody has to wonder where he stands.","feedback":"Well, this was supposed to be a nice little PR story. Penn gets worked up and calls President Bush a 'bloodsucking motherfucker,' and naturally that's now the only part anybody wants to talk about. So much for star power."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":123,"fields":{"question":31,"description":"Let's ask Gore Vidal. He's been around American politics and presidents longer than most of us have been alive. I'd rather have an interesting conversation than another celebrity photo-op.","feedback":"Vidal talks politics and history with you for a while, reporters get a few respectable quotes, and everybody goes home. I'm sure we locked down the aging Dick Cavett Show viewers, at least."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":124,"fields":{"question":32,"description":"Let's sit down and work it out. They don't want a messy convention any more than we do, and without a united party we're all screwed.","feedback":"The meeting is tense but productive. You give them a few platform assurances and promise to help the rest of the ticket. A few activists grumble, but everyone leaves the room understanding that there is still an election to win."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":125,"fields":{"question":32,"description":"We can listen, but I'm not giving away the campaign that got us here. They can ask for unity without asking us to become somebody else.","feedback":"Nobody storms out, but nobody hugs either. The convention is still yours, and the disagreement remains exactly what it was when everyone walked in."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":126,"fields":{"question":33,"description":"Running-mate follow-up placeholder.","feedback":""}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":127,"fields":{"question":33,"description":"Running-mate follow-up placeholder.","feedback":""}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":128,"fields":{"question":33,"description":"Running-mate follow-up placeholder.","feedback":""}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":129,"fields":{"question":33,"description":"Running-mate follow-up placeholder.","feedback":""}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":130,"fields":{"question":34,"description":"I would ask anyone in the media to actually do their due diligence and play the rest of the clip. They owe it to their audience to give context; anything short of that is dishonest.","feedback":"This has close to the opposite effect we wanted. When playing the full interview, viewers are treated to the part where you say Bill Maher should be your running mate. It's clear that part was a joke, but it just further feeds the narrative that you're unrealistic and not serious."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":131,"fields":{"question":34,"description":"Of course we would bring Osama bin Laden to justice! My point was that further expanding a disastrous war on terror through strikes that violate international law has gotten us nowhere.","feedback":"Over-explaining this may minimize the damage among the minority of Americans who actually care to hear your response, but the clip is still killing us with most Americans."}},{"model":"campaign_trail.answer","pk":132,"fields":{"question":34,"description":"I was making a larger point about the bloodlust and reckless militarism that has gotten our troops killed. This entire attitude that the United States is a moral authority over the entire globe is more harmful than any interview I've ever given. Let's be honest and say what we mean here.","feedback":"Well, maybe that'll help with people who care about international law and diplomacy. When talking about Osama bin Laden, that's not a lot of people. Expect this to hurt."}}];

